# Yank or archive a release

What to do on PyPI when a release turns out to be broken, or when the project is done. None of
this happens in the repository: these are actions on the index, taken by a project owner on
pypi.org.

## Pick the action

| Situation | Action | What installers do afterwards |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A release is broken, uninstallable, accidentally incompatible, or has a security hole | **Yank** it | Skip it, unless it is the only version matching an exact `==` / `===` pin |
| A file must not exist at all (a leaked secret inside the wheel, say) | **Delete** it | Cannot get it. PyPI never accepts that filename again, so a fixed file needs a new version |
| No more releases are planned | **Archive** the project | Install existing releases as before. PyPI refuses new uploads |

Yanking is the default answer to a bad release. It keeps every project that pinned the exact
version working, while new resolutions move past it. Deletion breaks those pins and cannot be
undone, so keep it for content that must not stay public, and rotate whatever leaked anyway.

## Yank a release

1. Release the fix first, so there is a version to move to.
2. Open <https://pypi.org/manage/project/{{ project_slug | replace('_', '-') }}/releases/>, choose *Options* next to the bad
   release, then *Yank*.
3. Give a reason. PyPI shows it on the release page and serves it to installers through the
   index API; pip, for one, prints it when a pin forces the yanked version. Say what to do
   instead, e.g. "Broken import on Python 3.12, use 1.4.1".

PyPI yanks whole releases, not single files. Un-yanking is the same menu.

## Archive the project

1. Make a final release whose README says the project is archived and why, and names an
   alternative if there is one. The README is the project description on PyPI, and the archive
   notice sits above it.
2. On the project's settings page on PyPI, use the *Project archival* section.

Existing releases stay installable; uploads are refused, so the release pipeline will fail if a
tag is pushed afterwards. Archiving can be undone from the same section.

## The status a project can have

The [project status markers](https://packaging.python.org/en/latest/specifications/project-status-markers/)
specification defines four. An index may implement any subset.

| Status | Meaning | New uploads | Downloads |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `active` | The default | Yes | Yes |
| `archived` | No updates expected | No | Yes |
| `deprecated` | Obsolete, maybe superseded | Yes | Yes |
| `quarantined` | Unsafe, e.g. malware; set by index administrators | No | No |

On PyPI, project owners can set `archived`; `quarantined` is applied by PyPI's administrators.

